# Centralized error handling

Handle Express errors in one place. Routes and services throw; they do not format HTTP errors themselves.

- Throw `AppError` with `statusCode`, `code`, and a safe `message`. Pass unexpected errors with `{ cause }`.
- Register `errorHandler` after every route. It is the only place that sends an error response.
- Response shape: `{ success: false, code, message }`. Log the stack on the server. Never send stacks, SQL, tokens, or passwords to the client.
- Async handlers must forward rejections (`next(err)` or an async wrapper). Do not use empty `catch` blocks.
- Map validation failures to `400`, auth failures to `401`, permission failures to `403`, missing records to `404`, and conflicts (duplicate vaccination) to `409`.

```js
// ❌ BAD
catch (err) {
  res.status(500).json({ error: err.message });
}

// ✅ GOOD
if (!child) throw new AppError(404, 'CHILD_NOT_FOUND', 'Child not found');
```

```js
export function errorHandler(err, _req, res, _next) {
  const status = err.statusCode ?? 500;
  const code = err.code ?? 'INTERNAL_ERROR';
  console.error({ code, err });
  res.status(status).json({
    success: false,
    code,
    message: status === 500 ? 'Something went wrong' : err.message,
  });
}
```
